The standard pattern in Spring Boot services is to declare a `Logger` as a `private static final` field at the top of each class. This is not just convention — it has a performance reason. Creating a logger inside a method allocates a new object on every invocation, and on hot paths handling thousands of requests per second, that object churn adds up. The static declaration creates the logger once when the class loads and reuses it for the lifetime of the JVM.

```java
@Service
public class OrderService {
    private static final Logger log = LoggerFactory.getLogger(OrderService.class);

    public Order createOrder(OrderRequest req) {
        log.info("order.creating customer={} amount={}", req.getCustomerId(), req.getAmount());
        Order order = processOrder(req);
        log.info("order.created orderId={} status={}", order.getId(), order.getStatus());
        return order;
    }
}
```

Notice the **parameterized logging** pattern: `log.info("customer={}", id)` instead of `log.info("customer=" + id)`. The difference matters under load. String concatenation builds the string even when INFO is disabled on that package. Parameterized logging skips the allocation entirely — the arguments are only resolved if the level is enabled. On a high-throughput service with DEBUG disabled, this saves thousands of unnecessary string concatenations per second.

The correlation ID itself comes from a servlet filter that runs before any controller logic. The filter checks for an incoming `X-Correlation-ID` header — if present, it propagates the upstream ID; if absent, it generates a new UUID. It stores the ID in MDC, passes it back in the response header, and crucially clears MDC in a `finally` block.

```java
@Component
@Order(Ordered.HIGHEST_PRECEDENCE)
public class CorrelationIdFilter extends OncePerRequestFilter {
    @Override
    protected void doFilterInternal(HttpServletRequest request,
                                    HttpServletResponse response,
                                    FilterChain chain) throws ServletException, IOException {
        String correlationId = Optional
                .ofNullable(request.getHeader("X-Correlation-ID"))
                .orElse(UUID.randomUUID().toString());

        MDC.put("correlationId", correlationId);
        response.setHeader("X-Correlation-ID", correlationId);
        try {
            chain.doFilter(request, response);
        } finally {
            MDC.remove("correlationId");  // Without this, Tomcat's thread reuse leaks the ID
        }
    }
}
```

The `finally` block is the most commonly missed detail. Tomcat uses a thread pool — when a request completes, its thread returns to the pool and serves a different request. If you do not clear MDC, the next request on that thread inherits the previous request's correlation ID, producing logs that look like two unrelated requests share the same session. This bug is subtle because it only manifests under concurrent load and makes log investigation actively misleading rather than just incomplete.

Spring Boot auto-instruments JVM metrics, HTTP request metrics, and database connection pool metrics out of the box. These are essential but they only tell you about infrastructure health. A drop in `orders.created` rate is often the first signal of a business-impacting failure — faster than JVM metrics and more specific than HTTP error rates.

```java
@Service
public class OrderService {
    private final Counter orderCounter;
    private final Timer orderTimer;

    public OrderService(MeterRegistry registry) {
        this.orderCounter = Counter.builder("orders.created")
                .description("Total orders successfully created")
                .register(registry);
        this.orderTimer = Timer.builder("orders.processing.time")
                .description("Order processing latency")
                .publishPercentileHistogram()   // enables fleet-wide P99 in PromQL
                .register(registry);
    }

    public Order createOrder(OrderRequest req) {
        return orderTimer.record(() -> {
            Order order = processOrder(req);
            orderCounter.increment();
            return order;
        });
    }
}
```

Inject `MeterRegistry` — the Micrometer facade — into any Spring bean to register custom instruments. The `publishPercentileHistogram()` call on the Timer is critical for multi-instance services: it publishes histogram buckets that Prometheus can aggregate across all pods using `histogram_quantile()`, giving you a fleet-wide P99 rather than a per-pod number that hides outliers.

Auto-instrumentation covers HTTP calls, JDBC queries, and messaging. But it does not know about your business logic. When a checkout operation calls an external payment gateway, the auto-instrumented span shows a generic HTTP call. Adding a custom span with business-specific tags makes the trace immediately useful for debugging.

```java
@Service
public class PaymentService {
    private final Tracer tracer;

    public PaymentResult charge(PaymentRequest req) {
        Span span = tracer.nextSpan().name("payment.charge").start();
        try (Tracer.SpanInScope scope = tracer.withSpan(span)) {
            span.tag("payment.provider", req.getProvider());
            span.tag("payment.amount", String.valueOf(req.getAmount()));
            PaymentResult result = paymentGateway.charge(req);
            span.tag("payment.status", result.getStatus());
            return result;
        } catch (Exception e) {
            span.error(e);  // marks the span as failed in the waterfall
            throw e;
        } finally {
            span.end();
        }
    }
}
```

The tags — provider, amount, status — let you filter traces in Jaeger by business context rather than just service name. When latency spikes only for one payment provider, filtering by `payment.provider=stripe` isolates the problem immediately. Without these tags, you would be scanning through thousands of traces manually looking for the slow ones.

The checkout handler makes four downstream calls. The code looks clean and readable — but every call blocks the thread until the response arrives, and the calls run in sequence even though some have no dependency on each other.

```java
@Service
public class CheckoutService {

    public CheckoutResult checkout(CheckoutRequest req) {
        InventoryResult inventory =
            inventoryClient.checkAvailability(req.getItems());          // ~800ms
        PricingResult pricing =
            pricingClient.calculatePrice(req.getItems(), req.getUser()); // ~800ms
        PaymentResult payment =
            paymentClient.charge(req.getPaymentMethod(), pricing.getTotal()); // ~800ms
        notificationClient.sendConfirmation(req.getUser());             // ~400ms
        return new CheckoutResult(inventory, pricing, payment);
    }
}
```

The latency is additive: 800 + 800 + 800 + 400 = 2,800ms. But inventory and pricing have no dependency on each other — they both need only the request items. The notification call does not affect the response at all, yet it blocks the thread for 400ms before the customer receives their checkout confirmation. These two architectural mistakes — sequential independent calls and synchronous fire-and-forget operations — are invisible in a code review but immediately obvious in a trace waterfall.

The fix addresses both findings from the trace analysis: parallel execution for independent calls and asynchronous publishing for the notification. Inventory and pricing have no data dependency between them — they both need only the original request items — so they can run concurrently. The notification has no effect on the checkout response, so it should be a fire-and-forget event rather than a blocking call.

```java
@Service
public class CheckoutService {
    private final ExecutorService executor =
        Executors.newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor();

    public CheckoutResult checkout(CheckoutRequest req) {
        CompletableFuture<InventoryResult> inventoryFuture =
            CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(
                () -> inventoryClient.checkAvailability(req.getItems()), executor);
        CompletableFuture<PricingResult> pricingFuture =
            CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(
                () -> pricingClient.calculatePrice(req.getItems(), req.getUser()), executor);

        InventoryResult inventory = inventoryFuture.join();
        PricingResult pricing = pricingFuture.join();

        PaymentResult payment =
            paymentClient.charge(req.getPaymentMethod(), pricing.getTotal());

        eventPublisher.publish(new OrderConfirmedEvent(req.getUser(), payment.getOrderId()));
        return new CheckoutResult(inventory, pricing, payment);
    }
}
```

The `supplyAsync` calls launch inventory and pricing checks concurrently on virtual threads, and `join()` waits for both results. Payment still runs sequentially because it depends on the pricing result. The notification publishes to Kafka and returns immediately — a consumer picks it up asynchronously without blocking the checkout response.

The new critical path is max(800, 800) + 800 = 1,600ms — a **43% reduction** from the original 2,800ms. The notification no longer appears in the response path at all. After deploying this fix, re-run the same Jaeger search for traces above 2,000ms. The new waterfall shows inventory and pricing spans overlapping in time, confirming that the parallelisation is working. This before-and-after comparison in the trace UI is the most convincing evidence you can present in a postmortem.
